# CommitOnce environment template.
#
# Copy to .env and edit. `.env` is gitignored; this file is committed and must never
# contain a real secret, a real keypair, or a real private RPC token.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# NOTHING IN THIS REPOSITORY LOADS THIS FILE AUTOMATICALLY. There is no dotenv dependency
# anywhere in the workspace, deliberately: the published SDK has zero runtime dependencies,
# and a build script that silently reads a file in the repository root is a supply-chain and
# reproducibility hazard. Export the values in the shell that runs the scripts, or load them
# explicitly:
#
#   set -a; . ./.env; set +a          # bash, exports everything in .env
#   bash scripts/build.sh
#
# Every variable below is read by something in this repository, and the comment above each
# one names what reads it and what happens when it is unset. Variables read only by the
# examples in examples/ are marked as such; they have no effect on the program build, the
# Rust suite, or the SDK suite.
#
# The examples submit real transactions to whatever cluster RPC_URL points at. Point it at
# devnet unless you intend to spend real SOL.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Toolchain location (WSL2 Ubuntu)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: scripts/build.sh, scripts/test.sh — both export it unconditionally, overriding
# whatever you set here. Listed for completeness, and commented out because sourcing this
# file with HOME set would change your shell's HOME.
#
# Why it matters: the Rust, Solana and Anchor toolchains live under this home
# (~/.cargo/bin, ~/.config/solana, the nvm-managed Node install). Running the scripts with a
# different HOME makes cargo look for its registry and toolchain in the wrong place and
# makes `anchor` disappear from PATH, which surfaces as `command not found` rather than as a
# clear error. The scripts also prepend /home/dell2u/solana-current/bin and $HOME/.cargo/bin
# to PATH; they do not read PATH from this file.
#
# HOME=/home/dell2u

# Read by: scripts/build.sh, scripts/test.sh (both default it to /home/dell2u/cot-target).
#
# Why it points at ext4: the repository lives on a Windows drive mounted into WSL. Cargo's
# incremental cache and the Solana linker write hundreds of megabytes of small files, and on
# that filesystem the build is unusably slow. Keeping the cache on native ext4 fixes it;
# scripts/build.sh copies the small artifacts (.so, IDL, generated types) back into
# <repo>/target so they stay inspectable from Windows.
#
# Note: Anchor's deploy directory follows this variable, so program keypairs are read from
# $CARGO_TARGET_DIR/deploy. scripts/build.sh force-copies deploy-keys/*-keypair.json into
# both $CARGO_TARGET_DIR/deploy and <repo>/target/deploy.
CARGO_TARGET_DIR=/home/dell2u/cot-target


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build overrides
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: scripts/build.sh. Default: v2.
#
# SBPFv2 is the default because LiteSVM 0.10.0 cannot verify a v3 ELF (e_flags = 0x3) and
# rejects it with Instruction(InvalidAccountData) — a v3 build would be a program this
# project's own test suite cannot execute. Set SBPF_ARCH=v3 only when the harness supports
# it; expect `bash scripts/test.sh` to fail until then.
SBPF_ARCH=v2

# Read by: scripts/test.sh (defaults to $CARGO_TARGET_DIR/deploy) and by the Rust integration
# tests in programs/commit-once/tests/common/mod.rs, which fall back to <repo>/target/deploy
# when it is unset. Point it at the directory holding commit_once.so and demo_counter.so.
# If neither location has the artifacts, the tests fail with an explicit "build the programs
# first" message — they never silently skip.
#
# COMMIT_ONCE_DEPLOY_DIR=/home/dell2u/cot-target/deploy


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RPC endpoints (examples only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: examples/sol-transfer, examples/spl-transfer, examples/custom-program,
# examples/jupiter-swap. REQUIRED by all four — they throw if it is empty.
# No script, test, or build step reads this. The Rust suite runs against LiteSVM in-process
# and never contacts a cluster.
#
# Use your own endpoint for anything beyond a smoke test: the public endpoints are rate
# limited and shared.
RPC_URL=https://api.devnet.solana.com

# Read by: the same four examples. Optional. Defaults to RPC_URL with the scheme rewritten
# from http:// to ws://. Only used for subscription-based confirmation (sendAndConfirm), so
# an endpoint that supports HTTP but not WebSocket still works for sending.
#
# RPC_WS_URL=wss://api.devnet.solana.com


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signer keypair (examples only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: the four examples, which accept either KEYPAIR or WALLET_PATH (KEYPAIR wins) and
# throw if both are unset. The signer is both the fee payer and the intent authority.
#
# The value is passed straight to fs.readFile, so `~` is NOT expanded: use an absolute path.
# This must be a Solana CLI keypair file — a JSON array of 64 byte values, as produced by
# `solana-keygen new`. Never commit it, never paste it into an issue, and use a throwaway
# devnet keypair rather than a key that holds anything of value.
KEYPAIR=/home/dell2u/.config/solana/id.json

# Alias for KEYPAIR, read by the same examples. Kept because some tooling exports this name.
#
# WALLET_PATH=/home/dell2u/.config/solana/id.json


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intent inputs (examples only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: examples/sol-transfer. REQUIRED. Base58 address that receives the transfer.
# RECIPIENT=

# Read by: examples/sol-transfer. REQUIRED. Integer lamports, not SOL. 1 SOL = 1_000_000_000.
# AMOUNT_LAMPORTS=

# Read by: examples/spl-transfer. REQUIRED. Base58 mint address of the SPL token.
# MINT=

# Read by: examples/spl-transfer and examples/jupiter-swap. REQUIRED by both.
# Integer base units, i.e. the raw amount before decimals are applied.
# AMOUNT_BASE_UNITS=

# Read by: examples/spl-transfer. REQUIRED. Base58 address that receives the tokens.
# RECIPIENT=

# Read by: examples/jupiter-swap. REQUIRED. Base58 mint of the token being sold.
# INPUT_MINT=

# Read by: examples/jupiter-swap. REQUIRED. Base58 mint of the token being bought.
# OUTPUT_MINT=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Intent identity (examples only)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: all four examples. Optional; default `order_928`.
#
# This is the idempotency key. It is the whole point of the example: run the example twice
# with the SAME ORDER_ID and the second run is blocked by the existing receipt instead of
# executing a second time. Change ORDER_ID and you are describing a different intent, which
# is allowed to commit.
#
# Do not include transport details (blockhash, priority fee, signature, attempt number,
# RPC URL) in anything that feeds the payload fingerprint — a retry must look like the same
# intent, or the guard cannot work. See docs/SECURITY_MODEL.md §5.
# ORDER_ID=order_928

# Read by: all four examples. Optional; defaults to no memo (null).
#
# Part of the payload fingerprint when set, so changing it changes the intent's identity and
# a retry with a different MEMO is reported as a conflict rather than treated as a duplicate.
# MEMO=invoice-928


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Jupiter-style swap example only
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: examples/jupiter-swap. Optional; defaults to null, in which case no slippage
# guard instruction is added. Integer basis points (50 = 0.50%).
# SLIPPAGE_BPS=50

# Read by: examples/jupiter-swap. Optional, and mutually exclusive in practice — the inline
# value takes precedence when both are set. A base64-encoded serialized swap transaction,
# supplied directly.
#
# This exists so the example can demonstrate the guard against a real swap transaction
# without shipping a Jupiter API client (the SDK has zero runtime dependencies by policy).
# JUPITER_SWAP_TX=

# Read by: examples/jupiter-swap. Optional. Path to a file containing the base64-encoded swap
# transaction described above. Whitespace around the contents is trimmed.
# JUPITER_SWAP_TX_FILE=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Anchor CLI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Read by: the Anchor CLI itself, not by any script in this repository. Anchor reads these as
# overrides for the [provider] section of Anchor.toml (cluster and wallet). Confirm the
# precedence against your installed anchor version before relying on it: Anchor.toml pins
# cluster = "localnet" and wallet = "~/.config/solana/id.json" today, and `anchor test` is
# not used by this project (skip_local_validator = true; the tests run through LiteSVM).
#
# ANCHOR_PROVIDER_URL=https://api.devnet.solana.com
# ANCHOR_WALLET=/home/dell2u/.config/solana/id.json


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reminders
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# * There is no mainnet deployment of commit_once. Do not point anything at mainnet-beta
#   expecting to find it.
# * There is no CI secret, no npm token, no deployment credential, and no API key that this
#   repository needs. If you think you need one, open an issue first — nothing here should
#   ever require a secret to build, test, or verify.
# * Never commit .env. It is gitignored, but the guard against a mistake is reading
#   `git status` before `git commit`.
